"Speeding Up Sum-Check Proving (Extended Version) (https://eprint.iacr.org/2026/587)\nciteturn10view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://eprint.iacr.org/2026/587\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 82\nL0: # What a lovely hat\nL1: #### Is it made out of 【0†tin foil†iacr.org】?\nL2: \nL3: 【1†Cryptology ePrint Archive】\nL4: \nL5:   * 【2†Papers】 Updates from the last:\nL6:     * 【3†7 days】\nL7:     * 【4†31 days】\nL8:     * 【5†6 months】\nL9:     * 【6†365 days】\nL10:     * * * *\nL11: \nL12:     * 【7†Listing by year】\nL13:     * 【8†All papers】\nL14:     * 【9†Compact view】\nL15:     * 【10†Subscribe†www.iacr.org】\nL16:     * * * *\nL17: \nL18:     * 【11†How to cite】\nL19:     * * * *\nL20:     * 【12†Harvesting metadata】\nL21:   * 【2†Submissions】\nL22:     * 【13†Submit a paper】\nL23:     * 【14†Revise or withdraw a paper】\nL24:     * 【15†Acceptance and publishing conditions】\nL25: \nL26: 【16†Image: Search Button】\nL27: \nL28: [Input] [Button: Search ]\nL29: #### Paper 2026/587\nL30: \nL31: ### 【17†Speeding Up Sum-Check Proving (Extended Version)】\nL32: \nL33: Quang Dao, Carnegie Mellon University\nL34: \nL35: Zachary DeStefano, New York University\nL36: \nL37: Suyash Bagad, Ingonyama\nL38: \nL39: Yuval Domb, Ingonyama\nL40: \nL41: Justin Thaler, Georgetown University, a16z crypto research\nL42: ##### Abstract\nL43: The sum-check protocol is a foundational primitive in modern cryptographic proof systems, but its prover-side cost has emerged as a concrete bottleneck. This paper introduces three complementary techniques that significantly reduce sum-check proving time and memory, especially in the context of zero-knowledge virtual machines (zkVMs).\nL44: First, for applications involving products of many multilinear polynomials, we develop a new algorithm that significantly reduces the number of field multiplications required for proving. Second, we develop a \"small-value sum-check prover\" algorithm. This significantly speeds up the prover in the common setting where the polynomials being summed evaluate to 64 or 32-bit integers, or to elements of a small sub-field within a larger extension field.\nL45: Even outside of the small-value setting, this algorithm yields a faster \"streaming prover\", by which we mean a small-space algorithm that applies whenever the terms being summed can be enumerated in small space (as arises, for example, in zkVM applications). Third, we nearly eliminate prover overhead in the ubiquitous case where one factor is an equality polynomial by exploiting its decomposable tensor structure.\nL46: We implement these techniques in Jolt, a state-of-the-art zkVM, and evaluate their performance. In Jolt, we observe over an order of magnitude runtime speedup and memory reduction on the Spartan sub-protocol, and $1.7\\times$ to $2.2\\times$ speedups for a key high-degree sum-check sub-protocol in the Shout batch-evaluation argument.\nL47: Note: This manuscript directly combines and improves upon results from prior works: 2025/1117, 2024/1046, and 2024/1210\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n[2610.08258] zkLLMPoT: Efficient Zero Knowledge Proof of Training for Large Language Models (https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.08258)\nciteturn10view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.08258\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 169\nL0: 【0†Search】 【1†Submit】 【2†Donate†info.arxiv.org】 【3†Log in】\nL1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · 【4†Advanced search】\nL5: \nL6: # Computer Science > Artificial Intelligence\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 6 Oct 2026 (【5†v1】), last revised 11 Oct 2026 (this version, v2)]\nL9: # Title:zkLLMPoT: Efficient Zero Knowledge Proof of Training for Large Language Models\nL10: \nL11: Authors:【6†Junkai Liang】, 【7†Zhanpeng Guo】, 【8†Pengfei Wu】, 【9†Qingni Shen】, 【10†Jiaheng Zhang】, 【11†Zhonghai Wu】, 【12†Haiyang Xue】, 【13†Shengfang Zhai】\nL12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled zkLLMPoT: Efficient Zero Knowledge Proof of Training for Large Language Models, by Junkai Liang and 7 other authors\nL14: \nL15: 【14†View PDF】 【15†HTML (experimental)】\nL16: > Abstract:Auditing the claimed outcomes of large language model (LLM) training is challenging when model weights and training data are private, while cryptographically proving the full training process is prohibitively expensive at Transformer scale. We present zkLLMPoT, a zero-knowledge framework that certifies auditor-defined properties of a trained checkpoint through forward evaluation rather than verification of its optimization trajectory.\nL17: zkLLMPoT includes 2 phases: 1) The trainer fixes the architecture and the model weights are committed. Then the auditor selects challenge sequences, preventing the trainer from modifying the checkpoint in response to the audit data. 2) Then the trainer proves the objective value attained by the committed model on those sequences. This formulation makes the certification cost independent of the number of training iterations, without revealing model weights or requiring access to private training data.\nL18: We build on sumcheck- and lookup-based arguments to certify Transformer computations, while supporting next-token loss and task-specific audit objectives. Across four model families, operator-level benchmarks yield proving times of 41-59 seconds for 1.1-1.5B-parameter models and 131 seconds at 13B for the covered operators, with verification below half a second at a sequence length of 512.\nL19: Comments:  | 18 pages, 4 figures, Preprint\nL20: Subjects:  | Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\nL21: MSC (Mathematical Subject Classification) classes:  | 94A60, 68T50\nL22: ACM (Association of Computing Machinery Classification) classes:  | E.3; I.2.6; I.2.7\nL23: Cite as:  | 【16†arXiv:2610.08258】 [cs.AI]\nL24:    | (or 【17†arXiv:2610.08258v2】 [cs.AI] for this version)\nL25:    | 【18†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2610.08258†doi.org】 arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL26: ## Submission history\nL27: \nL28: From: Junkai Liang [【19†view email】]\nL29: 【5†[v1]】 Tue, 6 Oct 2026 12:35:49 UTC (112 KB)\nL30: [v2] Sun, 11 Oct 2026 04:45:08 UTC (115 KB)\nL31: \nL32: Full-text links:\nL33: \nL34: ## Access Paper:\nL35: \nL36: View a PDF of the paper titled zkLLMPoT: Efficient Zero Knowledge Proof of Training for Large Language Models, by Junkai Liang and 7 other authors\nL37: \nL38:   * 【14†View PDF】\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCUDA Toolkit 13.4 Update 2 - Release Notes — Release Notes 13.4 documentation (https://docs.nvidia.com/cuda/cuda-toolkit-release-notes/index.html)\nciteturn10view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn6view0\",\"pattern\":\"2026\"}); Total lines: 1091\nL48: CUDA TILE-IR AS  | 13.4.92  | x86_64, arm64-sbsa, arm64 (Windows)  | Linux, Windows\nL49: CUDA cuBLAS  | 13.8.0.4  | x86_64, arm64-sbsa, arm64 (Windows)  | Linux, Windows\nL50: CUDA cuDLA  | 13.4.92  | arm64-sbsa, arm64 (Windows)  | Linux, Windows\nL51: CUDA cuFFT  | 12.4.0.43  | x86_64, arm64-sbsa, arm64 (Windows)  | Linux, Windows\nL52: CUDA cuFile  | 1.19.1.55  | x86_64, arm64-sbsa  | Linux\nL53: CUDA cuobjclient  | 1.3.1.55  | x86_64, arm64-sbsa  | Linux\nL54: CUDA cuRAND  | 10.4.4.72  | x86_64, arm64-sbsa, arm64 (Windows)  | Linux, Windows\nL55: CUDA cuSOLVER  | 12.3.5.3  | x86_64, arm64-sbsa, arm64 (Windows)  | Linux, Windows\nL56: CUDA cuSPARSE  | 12.8.6.72  | x86_64, arm64-sbsa, arm64 (Windows)  | Linux, Windows\nL57: CUDA NPP  | 13.2.0.58  | x86_64, arm64-sbsa, arm64 (Windows)  | Linux, Windows\nL58: CUDA nvFatbin  | 13.4.92  | x86_64, arm64-sbsa, arm64 (Windows)  | Linux, Windows\nL59: CUDA nvJitLink  | 13.4.92  | x86_64, arm64-sbsa, arm64 (Windows)  | Linux, Windows\nL60: CUDA nvJPEG  | 13.2.3.58  | x86_64, arm64